# Findings adjudicated and rejected, with the reason. One `<path>: <title>` per line.

# PR #69 (issue #60). Half of this does not hold: `followUpComplete` already requires
# `nextStep == null`, so a stale PENDING job blocks the completion marker on its own, with or
# without the paused flag. The other half is real but predates this PR and is independent of
# appointments: the job branch of the estimate does not mirror the handler's gates (agent.enabled,
# isTestSilenced, step existence, the episode check, shouldBotHandle), so a stale job shows a
# phantom countdown even with no appointment anywhere. That is a separate defect in the estimate
# itself and belongs in its own change, not folded into the appointment flag. Filed as #72.
src/modules/conversations/service.ts: Validate pending jobs before marking the sequence paused

# PR #76 (issue #65). The premise is right — the guardrail never sees the picture — but neither
# remedy is. Submitting bytes to it means a multimodal guardrail: the guardrails agent is text-only
# by construction (toxicity / unsafe content / competitor mentions / prompt adherence, all over a
# string), so this is a feature of its own, with its own model, cost and latency on the hot path.
# And suppressing images whenever they cannot be screened would disable the tool precisely for the
# operator careful enough to turn guardrails on, which inverts the incentive. An image already has
# its own mandatory, default-closed control: `sendImage.allowedHosts` is an operator decision, empty
# by default, and empty refuses every call — no image leaves without an explicit choice about its
# ORIGIN. What was genuinely missing is that the operator could not know the split, so the hint on
# that field now says guardrails read text and never the picture.
src/graph/runtime.ts: Screen image contents before delivering guarded output

# PR #75 (issue #61). Real, and it predates this PR: `updateMirror` writes status/assignee and never
# `lastEventAt`, so the guard this change replaces (`existing.lastEventAt > newLastEventAt`) did not
# reject an in-flight conversation event after a console write either. The one sub-case where the old
# guard did reject it — a message landed in between and moved `lastEventAt` — is the exact discard
# that leaves a delayed handoff lost forever, i.e. issue #61 itself; the two cannot both hold.
# Closing it needs the version our own write produced, and the two endpoints it calls do not return
# it (`assignments#create` renders the agent; `toggle_status` renders success/conversation_id/
# current_status/snoozed_until). The REST `show` DOES render it, though
# (`conversations/partials/_conversation.json.jbuilder`), so the fix is a re-read after the write
# rather than a fork change — one extra call per console action, which is its own scope. The
# marker-based attempt at doing it without any version was built and removed inside this PR after
# review found four races in it. Filed as #77.
src/modules/chatwoot/mirror.ts: Fence source events after locally written state
src/modules/chatwoot/state-order.ts: Fence source events after locally written state

# PR #75 (issue #61). The gap is real — while the handoff's own event is delayed, the mirror does not
# know about the human, and a turn already running rechecks the ROW — but the remedy is not available
# yet. Trusting a versioned message snapshot for the assignee undoes a take-over made from our own
# console: that write claims no version, so the row sits ahead of its marks and the tail carries a
# HIGHER version with an OLDER truth. Measured, not argued: applying the suggested change turns "a
# take-over in the console survives a message tail from before it" red. Both failure modes end the
# same way (the bot answers over a human); this one is bounded by the webhook's own retry budget
# (AgentBots::WebhookJob, 3 attempts 3s apart), while the console one lasts until the echo arrives.
# #77 is what unblocks it: once a local write claims the version it produced (the REST show renders
# `updated_at`), the row is never ahead of its marks and a newer versioned snapshot stops being
# ambiguous. Then this change becomes correct, and desirable.
src/modules/chatwoot/mirror.ts: Mirror newer human ownership from message events
src/modules/chatwoot/state-order.ts: Mirror newer human ownership from message events


# PR #75 (issue #61). Real but out of scope for a mirror fix, and below the bar for the remedy it
# asks for (a drain / two-phase rollout). It needs an operator running MULTIPLE web replicas (the
# documented model is one replica, docs/deploy.md "Single replica (or one leader)"), a rolling
# deploy, and a webhook delivered late inside the overlap window — which Chatwoot bounds to roughly
# nine seconds (AgentBots::WebhookJob retries 3× at 3s). The failure is a stale status/assignee that
# the next conversation event repairs, which is the same self-healing behavior any out-of-order
# delivery already has. Making the rollout itself two-phase to close it would put deploy ceremony
# into every future release for a seconds-wide window.
src/modules/chatwoot/mirror.ts: Guard the watermark during rolling deploys

# PR #75 (issue #61), round 3. Accepted and implemented, then withdrawn on the evidence of round 4:
# letting an unordered message snapshot PROMOTE the assignee to a human fixes a lost handoff, and
# creates the mirror image of it — a message the human sent before the conversation went back to the
# bot arrives with the same last_activity_at and re-takes it, firing a false conversation.handoff at
# every subscriber. Without a version neither direction can be ordered, so the mirror keeps what it
# has: on a Chatwoot older than 4.0.2 a handoff whose events are all delayed past the human's first
# message stays bot-owned until the next conversation event repairs it. The supported deployments
# ship our fork (4.11.x), where the version exists and decides.
src/modules/chatwoot/mirror.ts: Preserve human-message handoffs without a version stamp

# PR #83 (issue #72), round 3. Real, and it predates this PR: once the scheduler claims the dead job
# and the handler drops it, `abandoned` goes back to false and the console draws its completion
# marker. That is exactly what it drew in the same state BEFORE this change (with no pending row the
# estimate branch already yielded nextStep null, and the marker only needs lastFollowUpAt). What this
# PR removes is the window where a LIVE COUNTDOWN was displayed for a follow-up that was going to be
# dropped, which is the defect #72 reports.
#
# Telling "the sequence finished" from "the sequence was cut short" after the job is gone needs a fact
# nothing stores: which step last fired, against the configured total. `Conversation.lastFollowUpAt`
# is a timestamp, not an index, and the job row that carried `stepIndex` is precisely what just
# disappeared. So the honest fix is a persisted marker (an aborted-at stamp, or the last step index),
# a schema change with its own migration and its own UI copy — a separate change, not a round of this
# one.
src/modules/conversations/service.ts: Persist abandonment after the scheduler consumes the job

# PR #87 (issue #74). The mechanism is real, the consequence is not. @langchain/openai only replays
# a reasoning item under ZDR when `reasoning.encrypted_content` came back
# (converters/responses.js:976), so with `store: false` the item is sometimes dropped from the
# replay — but that is what makes the follow-up VALID, not what breaks it: nothing carries an id the
# stateless request cannot resolve. Measured live, not argued. One tool round, capturing the second
# request's input: gpt-5.6-luna/high and gpt-5.4-mini/medium replay
# [message, reasoning, function_call, function_call_output], gpt-5.6-luna/low replays
# [message, function_call, function_call_output], and all three return the correct final answer. And
# through the real buildAgentGraph loop with a tool the agent must call three times, both
# gpt-5.6-luna/high and gpt-5.4-mini/medium complete with the right reply. So "OpenAI rejects the
# function-call output follow-up and no final reply is produced" does not happen.
#
# Requesting encrypted reasoning would preserve reasoning CONTINUITY across turns, which is a
# quality argument with no measurement behind it, and it would persist an opaque blob per turn in
# our own checkpointer. That is a mechanism justified by a hypothesis; if a measurable quality gap
# shows up on multi-step tool loops, it earns its own change.
src/graph/models.ts: Include encrypted reasoning in stateless tool loops

# PR #91. The window is real and can be long (the image job is a multi-arch matrix and its runner
# queue has held a job for hours), but the conclusion does not follow. At the instant `released`
# fires, the version is already public: the GitHub Release exists, with its notes and its source
# tarball. The reference tracks the RELEASE, which is the announcement of the version, not the
# container artifact, so a GHCR image lagging its own release is a gap between the image and the
# release, and putting the docs inside that gap does not close it. The remedy costs real mechanism:
# `workflow_run` runs in the default branch's context and carries none of the release payload (the
# tag would have to be re-resolved from the triggering run), `publish_github_package.yml` also has a
# `workflow_dispatch` trigger so the docs would fire on builds that are not releases, and it adds a
# failure mode that does not exist today (image published, docs silently not). A release whose image
# never builds is a broken release: repair the build or cut another tag. `workflow_dispatch` already
# covers repairing the page by hand.
.github/workflows/deploy-swagger.yml: Wait for image publication before deploying the docs

# PR #93. The mechanism is real: `bun dev` is `bun --hot`, the holder lives on `globalThis` precisely
# to survive a reload, and a reloaded module looking under the new key cannot see the timer parked
# under the old one — so a developer who hot-loads this commit gets a second scheduler tick until the
# process restarts. Two things bound it. It cannot reach production: a deploy is a new process with an
# empty `globalThis`, so there is no old holder to orphan. And in dev the doubled tick is benign by
# design, not by luck — the claim is `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` and `worker.ts` already states the
# invariant ("still correct if briefly doubled"): the singleton avoids a ghost timer, it is not what
# makes the claim correct. So the cost is some wasted polling in one dev process, cleared by the
# restart that pulling a structural change already implies.
#
# The remedy is what settles it. Both forms the finding offers reintroduce `secv4` into the code:
# keeping the keys stable is declining the change, and migrating the old holders means shipping
# `Symbol.for("secv4.…")` (or a sweep over `globalThis` symbols matching the old prefix) to be read
# forever, or as scheduled debt nobody deletes. That trades a transient dev inconvenience for the
# permanent presence of the exact string this change exists to remove. The PR body tells developers
# to restart `bun dev`, which closes the only real gap here (not knowing) at no cost in code.
src/modules/scheduler/worker.ts: Keep the legacy Symbol key during hot reloads
